$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$  EXTERNAL DNS $$$$$$$$$$$$$$$$

ExternalDNS is a tool that automatically creates and updates your domain name (DNS) entries for your apps running in Kubernetes or cloud.

==> STeps to implement External DNS
🚀 Step-by-step implementation
1. Create Azure DNS Zone
Create in Azure Portal:
example.com
Note:
Resource Group name
DNS Zone name
2. Enable AKS Managed Identity

If creating new AKS:

az aks create \
  --enable-managed-identity

If existing cluster:

az aks update \
  -g <rg> -n <aks-name> \
  --enable-managed-identity
3. Get AKS Managed Identity details
az aks show -g <rg> -n <aks-name> --query identity

You’ll get:

Principal ID (important)
4. Assign DNS permissions to AKS identity

Give AKS identity access to Azure DNS:

az role assignment create \
  --assignee <AKS-Principal-ID> \
  --role "DNS Zone Contributor" \
  --scope /subscriptions/<sub-id>/resourceGroups/<dns-rg>/providers/Microsoft.Network/dnszones/example.com
5. Enable AKS to use Workload Identity (recommended modern approach)
az aks update \
  -g <rg> -n <aks-name> \
  --enable-oidc-issuer \
  --enable-workload-identity
6. Create Kubernetes Service Account (linked to Managed Identity)
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: external-dns
  namespace: kube-system
  annotations:
    azure.workload.identity/client-id: "<managed-identity-client-id>"
7. Install ExternalDNS (Azure provider + Workload Identity)
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: external-dns
  namespace: kube-system
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: external-dns
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: external-dns
    spec:
      serviceAccountName: external-dns
      containers:
      - name: external-dns
        image: registry.k8s.io/external-dns/external-dns:v0.14.2
        args:
          - --source=ingress
          - --provider=azure
          - --registry=txt
          - --txt-owner-id=aks-externaldns
          - --azure-resource-group=<dns-rg>
          - --azure-subscription-id=<subscription-id>
8. Create Ingress with hostname annotation
metadata:
  annotations:
    external-dns.alpha.kubernetes.io/hostname: app.example.com
9. Apply workload identity binding (important step)

Link Kubernetes ServiceAccount → Azure Identity:

az identity federated-credential create

This connects:

AKS OIDC issuer
Kubernetes Service Account
Azure Managed Identity
10. Deploy app + ingress

Same as normal Kubernetes:

Deployment
Service
Ingress

ExternalDNS picks hostname automatically.
